# Rate Limit Tester

Checks whether an endpoint (login, OTP verification, password reset,
etc.) has rate-limiting or lockout protection, by sending a small,
fixed number of **identical** requests and watching for a change in
behavior — HTTP 429, a lockout message, or a CAPTCHA appearing.

## Why identical requests, not a guess list
This tool sends the **same request body every time** — it is not a
credential/OTP brute-forcer. The point is purely to test whether
throttling exists at all. A missing rate limit is itself a reportable
finding (OWASP API4:2019 — Unrestricted Resource Consumption) without
ever needing a correct guess to land.

## ⚠️ Authorized use only
Only use against systems you own or have explicit written permission
to test. Keep `--count` reasonable — this is a detection test, not a
load test or an attack.

## Requirements
```
pip install requests
```

## Usage
```bash
# Form-encoded login endpoint
python rate_limit_tester.py --url https://target.com/login \
  --data "user=test&pass=wrongpass123"

# JSON API, e.g. OTP verification
python rate_limit_tester.py --url https://target.com/api/otp/verify \
  --json '{"otp":"000000"}' --header "Content-Type: application/json" --count 20

# Slower pace (some apps rate-limit by requests/minute, not burst)
python rate_limit_tester.py --url https://target.com/login \
  --data "user=test&pass=wrong" --count 15 --delay 2
```

## What it detects
- `HTTP 429 Too Many Requests`
- `401`/`403` responses containing lockout-style language
- Response body keywords: "too many attempts", "rate limit", "try
  again later", "account locked", "captcha", "slow down", etc.

## Notes
- If no signal appears, that's a **potential finding**, not a
  guarantee — some apps rate-limit silently (identical response, but
  the IP/account gets flagged server-side for later action) or only
  kick in after a much higher threshold than tested. Always note this
  caveat in a report rather than claiming certainty.
- `--delay` matters: some rate limiters count requests per minute, so
  a fast burst might trigger it while a slower pace might not (or
  vice versa) — worth testing both.

## Status
Part of a personal 100-tool security scripting project. Verified
against two local mock servers: one that returns 429 after 5 attempts
(correctly detected at attempt 6), and one with no rate limiting at
all (correctly flagged as a potential finding after all 10 requests).

